      "content": "i did check the constant and the radial measurements, they're solid. the issue is that when i plug in the seismic data suggesting the moon's crustal thickness, the math just doesn't hold up for a solid core. if i use the gravitational pull data alone, the volume/mass ratio only makes sense if 70% of the interior is effectively vacuum or a very low-density manufactured substance. it's not just a \"discrepancy,\" it looks like a structural impossibility for a natural rock. if we assume the gravity readings are the absolute truth, what kind of shell thickness and material density would we be looking at to maintain that orbital stability? it feels like the only way the numbers work is if it's an artificial structure.",
